    {
      "id": "nursery-essentials",
      "title": "Nursery Essentials",
      "sub": "Organized by station · nights run from your bedroom, not the nursery",
      "guide": null,
      "info": [
        {
          "id": "nursery-essentials-the-key-call",
          "title": "The key call",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "The AAP recommends baby sleep in **your room**, on a separate surface, ideally for the first 6 months. So nights run from your bedroom, not the office/nursery. \"Keep essentials close by\" means building stations where the work happens — a bedside night station plus the main changing station — so nobody walks down the hall at 3 a.m."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "2a",
          "title": "2A. Bedside night station (your bedroom)"
        },
        {
          "id": "2b",
          "title": "2B. Changing station (office/nursery)"
        },
        {
          "id": "2c",
          "title": "2C. Cloth diaper system (quantities for full-time cloth)",
          "intro": "Newborns change 10–12×/day. With washing every 2 days:",
          "infoAfter": [
            {
              "id": "nursery-essentials-2c-material-honesty",
              "title": "Material honesty",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Most covers are PUL (polyester laminated with polyurethane). It doesn't touch skin — the cotton does — but wool covers are the only fully natural option."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "nursery-essentials-2c-prep-timing",
              "title": "Prep timing",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Natural fibers must be washed 3–5+ times hot to reach full absorbency. Prep the whole stash by **week 36 (~mid-April 2027)**."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "nursery-essentials-2c-rough-cost",
              "title": "Rough cost",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "~$500–800 all-in for this system, versus roughly $2,000–3,000 for disposables through potty training. Estimate only."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "title": "2D. Skin care & lotion",
          "infoBefore": [
            {
              "id": "nursery-essentials-2d-mechanism-first",
              "title": "Mechanism first",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "Newborn skin rarely needs lotion. The peeling in the first 2 weeks is normal shedding, not dryness. Two large trials of daily emollients from birth (BEEP, PreventADALL) found no eczema prevention, and BEEP saw slightly more skin infections. So: **spot-treat dry patches only.** Olive oil specifically weakens the skin barrier in infant studies — avoid it. Sunflower seed oil has the better barrier profile (evidence modest)."
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "infoAfter": [
            {
              "id": "nursery-essentials-2d-homemade-balm-why-a-balm-not-a-l",
              "title": "Homemade balm — why a balm, not a lotion",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "p",
                  "text": "anything with water in it grows bacteria and mold without a preservative. A water-free balm doesn't."
                },
                {
                  "type": "ul",
                  "items": [
                    "2 parts shea butter (unrefined) · 2 parts sunflower seed oil · 1 part beeswax pastilles, by weight",
                    "Melt in a double boiler, pour into sterilized glass jars, cool, lid.",
                    "Small batches; use within ~3 months. No essential oils. Patch-test on a small spot first.",
                    "Grass-fed tallow balm is a common alternative — evidence is anecdotal, same patch-test rule.",
                    "Balms and oils cause cloth-diaper repelling — use a paper liner when applied to the diaper area."
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "title": "2E. Feeding corner"
        },
        {
